INT. COMMUNITY CENTER LOCKER ROOM - NIGHT

Dented lockers, wet benches, a whiteboard with a half-erased play. The fluorescent tubes buzz.

COACH RUTHANNE PIKE, 50s, whistle around her neck, paces the length of the room. ZEKE, 20s, taped wrists, sits head down. DOM, 30s, pulls on a jersey. MEI-LING, 20s, goalie, rocks on her toes.

COACH
(to all)
Third period. We're down two.

ZEKE
We're down three.

COACH
Down two. The ref owes us one.

DOM
He owes us two.

MEI-LING
(muttering)
Ref owes us a kidney.

COACH
Zeke, look at me. Look up.

He lifts his head.

COACH (CONT'D)
You're the fastest man on this ice.

ZEKE
My knee.

COACH
Your knee's fine.

DOM
Coach, the knee isn't fine.

COACH
Dom, tie your skates.

DOM
They're tied.

COACH
Tie them again.

Mei-Ling punches her pads, twice. The sound is a heartbeat.

MEI-LING
Nobody gets past me. Not one.

COACH
That's what I like to hear.

The horn sounds down the hall. They rise as one. Zeke limps, then doesn't.
